            Common Mistakes When Choosing Between Milwaukee and DeWalt
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands make excellent tools, and choosing between them on individual tool reviews misses what actually determines your experience.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Single Tools Instead of Platforms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                You are not buying a drill, you are buying into a battery ecosystem you will be in for a decade. Which brand covers the specific tools you will want in three years matters far more than which drill wins a head-to-head today.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Batteries Are Interchangeable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                M18 and 20V MAX packs are not compatible in either direction, and third-party adapters risk warranties and create genuine safety issues. Splitting a workshop across both platforms means maintaining two sets of batteries and chargers permanently.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Local Service and Availability</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Warranty turnaround, parts availability, and which brand your local supplier actually stocks vary enormously by region and matter more than a small spec difference. A tool you can get serviced next week beats one you ship away for a month.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Kits for the Discount</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Combo kits look like value and routinely include a tool you will never use plus compact batteries you will outgrow. Once you own two good high-capacity packs, buying bare tools and choosing exactly what you need almost always works out cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
